tenum3.pp	gap: {$SCOPEDENUMS} + Enum.Element syntax, and `uses typinfo`. The subrange-of-enum half is FIXED (2026-09-05) -- the wall moved from `unknown type: first` to `undefined variable (TRange1)`, i.e. from parsing the type to USING its name.
tenumerators1.pp	gap: Classes TList/TFPList GetEnumerator + custom enumerator classes
tforin1.pp	gap: for-in over a subrange/enum TYPE NAME rather than a variable -- `for x in IntRange do`. The subrange TYPE now parses (2026-09-05); the wall moved to `undefined variable (IntRange)`, which is the for-in-over-a-type form, not the subrange.
tforin24.pp	gap: enumerator SELECTION by loop-var type (exit 0 with wrong output — keep skipped; re-confirmed 2026-07-15)
tforin2.pp	gap: for-in over a Classes.TStringList (its GetEnumerator), Track B. The STRING half now works: a call-result container and the `operator enumerator` for String both compile — the refusal moved from line 60 to line 75
tforin3.pp	wontfix: a nil GetEnumerator. fpc emits a nil guard and treats the for-in as an empty loop (this test exists to assert that); pxx reports `Runtime error 216 (nil reference)` and stops. MEASURED that ours is the deliberate emitted nil-check and not a fault: with --no-nil-check the same program SEGFAULTS, so the 216 is feature-a-emitted-nil-checks turning a fault into a located report, which is the check working. CHOSEN, not tolerated: a GetEnumerator returning nil is a mistake in the source -- an empty collection returns an enumerator whose MoveNext is False, not nil -- and the goal doc says an input only produced by a mistake makes FPC's answer not a specification, and to prefer the answer that leaves the mistake visible. Note the alternative is not benign: this test's own MoveNext returns True unconditionally, so "call it anyway" is an infinite loop, and fpc only escapes because it skips the loop entirely. REOPEN on real source that returns nil from GetEnumerator MEANING empty; an fpc test asserting fpc is not that
tforin8.pp	gap: IEnumerable/IEnumerator interfaces driving for-in
tforin9.pp	gap: `enumerator MoveNext` / `enumerator Current` member DIRECTIVES on an interface declaring its own enumerator protocol. `operator Enumerator(AList: TMyList): IMyIterator` itself parses
tgeneric102.pp	gap: specialize of generic class declared in another unit (inline class funcs)
tgeneric107.pp	gap: inline specialize expression (specialize G<T>.Create as expression/procvar)
tgeneric16.pp	gap: ClassName of a class inheriting a specialization. The virtual-dispatch half of this row is fixed (an override implemented before its base kept losing its VMT slot); the ONE remaining line is the first: fpc prints the specialized parent `TAdvStack<System.LongInt>` where pxx prints the declared child name `TIntegerStack`. Every other line now matches fpc byte for byte. Check the goal doc before burning: a differing name is closer to a differing diagnostic than to a wrong value, and pxx does not chase FPC parity
tgeneric23.pp	wontfix: requires FPC `fgl` unit as shipped (TFPGList)
tgeneric4.pp	wontfix: dialect-pass -- pxx compiles this %FAIL row and RUNS IT CORRECTLY. MEASURED 2026-09-06: prints `Unit`, exit 0, which is what the row's OWN assertion demands (`if slist.data<>'Unit' then halt(1)`); on pin v404 it printed `Program` and halted 1, the silent wrong answer bug-p-a-generic-template-body-resolves-its-symbols-at-the-specialization-site fixed. The `{ %fail }` records an FPC LIMITATION its own comment states -- "should found the LocalFill in ugeneric4, but for the moment that is not allowed since the assembler symbol is not global" -- not a rule the language wants; accepting what FPC rejects is not a defect (CLAUDE.md). REGRESSION ASSERTION: test_gen_declunit26 (test/test_generic_body_binds_in_its_declaring_unit.pas), whose row 1 fails if the program's copy ever wins again.
tgeneric26.pp	wontfix: dialect-pass — a type parameter in a VARIANT part. fpc forbids it; pxx supports it and MEASURED correct, which is what separates this from the other accepts-invalid rows: `specialize TRecArr<Integer>` compiles, `a[0].F := 7` then reads `a[0].Z` as 7, i.e. the variant cases alias as a variant must. Verified for internal consistency only -- fpc rejects the source, so there is no oracle to diff against, and that limit is the reason this says what was checked rather than 'works'. Accepting what FPC rejects is not a defect (the goal doc), and here the accepted construct does the right thing rather than merely parsing
